{"id":4342,"date":"2021-11-22T17:56:10","date_gmt":"2021-11-23T01:56:10","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4342"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:42","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:42","slug":"lesson-2-keyboard-remote-2","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lesson-2-keyboard-remote-2\/","title":{"rendered":"Lesson 2: Keyboard Remote"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd18077\" name=\"6a2214bd18077\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote Control vs Autonomous<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]What does it mean for a robot to be autonomous or remote controlled? An autonomous robot is a robot that can perform an action automatically without being directly controlled by a person. These robots are usually able to use their sensors to gain information and make decisions! A remote controlled robot is a robot that has a person directly controlling what the robot does with a remote controller.<\/p>\n<p>Try taking the \u201cAutonomous or Not?\u201d quiz below. You will see a a bunch of different robots and answer which you think is an autonomous robot or a remote controlled robot!<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/11\/Copy-of-Autonomous-or-Not_.pdf\" class=\"pdfemb-viewer\" style=\"\" data-width=\"max\" data-height=\"max\" data-mobile-width=\"500\"  data-scrollbar=\"none\" data-download=\"on\" data-tracking=\"on\" data-newwindow=\"on\" data-pagetextbox=\"off\" data-scrolltotop=\"off\" data-startzoom=\"100\" data-startfpzoom=\"100\" data-toolbar=\"bottom\" data-toolbar-fixed=\"off\">Copy-of-Autonomous-or-Not_<br\/><\/a>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd18317\" name=\"6a2214bd18317\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pseudocode<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Pseudocode is a helpful way to keep your code organized and it can help with planning big projects like a keyboard remote! Here is an example of how pseudocode might be used.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">if I enter \"w\"\r\nthen my drone will fly up towards the ceiling for 1 second<\/pre>\n<p>Can you think of any other buttons we might need to build a keyboard remote? What flight direction do you think your drone should go for each button?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd183fe\" name=\"6a2214bd183fe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">User input is also great for remote controlling your drone! In this activity, you will program your keyboard to act as a remote, with \u201cwasd\u201d and \u201cijkl\u201d as the joysticks.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, and then create the drone object and pair your drone.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, include print commands for \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Command options<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201d and then for each of the eight flight directions: positive and negative roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Make sure to include a \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">quit<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201d option, which will land the drone.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"Command Options: \")\r\n\r\nprint(\"w: throttle up \\n\"\r\n\r\n    \"s: throttle down\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"a: yaw left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"d: yaw right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"i: pitch forward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"k: pitch backward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"j: roll left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"l: roll right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"q: quit\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd18468\" name=\"6a2214bd18468\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have your drone take off, and then set your trim if needed. This is for if your drone is drifting and you want to fix its pitch or roll, so if you\u2019re not having that issue, you can skip this step.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, create a variable called power and set its value to 40. This way, the default power will be 40%.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_trim(0,0) # Adjust for your drone\r\n\r\npower = 40<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd184da\" name=\"6a2214bd184da\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set up a while loop so that while true, the program can take user input to make decisions. Next, reset the roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">direction<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> &#8220;Input a command: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a letter, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while True:\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 direction = input(\"Input a command: \")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bd1856b\" name=\"6a2214bd1856b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like the user input menu, you need to use if, else if, and else statements to have your drone fly in different directions based on the user input. For the w key, use an if statement and then include a command for your drone to throttle up. In the parameters, use the power variable, which will have the drone move at 40% power.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    if direction == \"w\":\r\n\r\n        drone.set_throttle(power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For the other 7 flight commands, use else if statements. If you want the drone to go to the left, down, or backwards, put a negative sign next to the power variable in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\"> \u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"s\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0     drone.set_throttle(-power)\r\n\r\n \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"a\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"d\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_yaw(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"i\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"k\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"j\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_roll(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif direction == \"l\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0drone.set_roll(power)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Write one more else if statement for the quit option. This will wait so the landing command isn\u2019t skipped, land the drone, and break out of the loop.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif direction == \"q\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0     time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Include an option that will let the user know they made a mistake if they input something that isn\u2019t an option in the program.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a command\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, include a delay and a move command with one second in the parameters. This is what will actually move the drone, along with the code you wrote for each key!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Outside of the loop, include a print command so you know when you\u2019ve exited the program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Remote Control vs Autonomous [vc_column_text]What does it mean for a robot to be autonomous or remote controlled? An autonomous robot is a robot that can perform an&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lesson-2-keyboard-remote-2\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4346,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2119,2121,2122,2134,2111,2136],"class_list":["post-4342","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-user-input","module-seasonal","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4342","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4342\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9110,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4342\/revisions\/9110"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4346"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4342"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4342"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4342"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}